5391: 
5392: \paragraph{Case-evidence protocol.}
5393: The four case-study rows below are not additional mathematical theorems; they
5394: are the path-verified protocol records used to instantiate
5395: Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion}.  A row is complete for this purpose when
5396: it names the source or artifact surface, the invariant that failed, the gate
5397: decision, and the safe manuscript lesson.  For the present package the row
5398: protocol is:
5399: \begin{itemize}
5400: \item \emph{Newmath intake isolation}: the intake-seed path and active-paper
5401: detector show that a candidate remains inactive preparation until human
5402: promotion creates \path{2026_*}, \path{main.tex}, and \path{PIPELINE.md}.
5403: \item \emph{Upper-fibers overlap block}: the route identity, submitted sibling,
5404: overlap ledgers, and program-board state show the recorded
5405: \path{deferred_wait_for_prior_submission} decision and the finite next-action
5406: set of closure, merge, supersession, or waiting for prior-route feedback.
5407: \item \emph{Fake-extension block}: the phase-D hard-lint script and
5408: this paper's artifact inventory show a theorem-content-gate rejection of file
5409: churn or compilation that did not add load-bearing theorem content.
5410: \item \emph{Rule110 limitation gate}: the status file, collision-audit ledger,
5411: exhaustiveness ledger, and paper-data reference show a bounded limitation that
5412: must be disclosed or must block artifact claims; they do not show fresh dynamic
5413: validation.
5414: \end{itemize}
5415: Thus the rows prove path completeness and reviewability only in the operational
5416: sense of Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion}; they do not certify fresh
5417: daemon execution, full-source rebuilds, or Rule110 reruns.
5418: 
5419: \begin{proposition}[Review-bundle case-snapshot coverage]
5420: \label{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}
5421: For the four case-study rows in Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}, the review
5422: bundle contains a finite external-review surface for every local case path used
5423: by the paper.  The Newmath block has two local paths, the upper-fibers block has
5424: five local paths, the fake-extension block has two local paths, and the Rule110
5425: block has four local paths, for thirteen local-path strings after resolving this
5426: paper's relative \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md} to its absolute path in the paper
5427: directory.  The manifest file
5428: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} has one
5429: \path{case_evidence} entry for each case row and, inside each entry, a
5430: \path{reviewable_snapshots} list matching those local paths.  File-valued local
5431: surfaces are copied into \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/}; directory-valued
5432: local surfaces are represented there by directory file-manifests.  Consequently
5433: the case-study claim made by this paper is externally reviewable at the
5434: path-verified snapshot level.  It is not a command-run validation claim, a fresh
5435: full-source rebuild claim, a daemon-execution claim, or a fresh Rule110 dynamic
5436: rerun claim.
5437: \end{proposition}
5438: 
5439: \begin{proof}
5440: The path catalogue displayed at the beginning of this section is finite and
5441: partitions the case surfaces into the four named rows: two paths for Newmath
5442: intake isolation, five paths for the upper-fibers overlap block, two paths for
5443: the fake-extension block, and four paths for the Rule110 limitation gate.  The
5444: manifest row for each case repeats exactly the same local-path list in its
5445: \path{local_paths} field and attaches a \path{reviewable_snapshots} list.  In
5446: that list the Newmath row names
5447: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/newmath_intake_seed_directory_manifest.json}
5448: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/newmath_pipeline_auto.py}; the
5449: upper-fibers row names
5450: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/upper_fibers_route_directory_manifest.json},
5451: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/upper_fibers_SUBMITTED},
5452: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/upper_fibers_semantic_overlap_blockers.json},
5453: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/upper_fibers_semantic_overlap_blockers.md},
5454: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/program_board_machine.md}; the
5455: fake-extension row names
5456: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/fake_extension_phase_d_lint.py} and
5457: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/fake_extension_artifact_inventory.md}; and the
5458: Rule110 row names \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_STATUS.md},
5459: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_collision_audit_findings.md},
5460: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_exhaustiveness_audit_findings.md},
5461: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_papers_research_report.md}.
5462: These thirteen bundle paths are in bijection with the thirteen local-path
5463: strings.  The two directory-valued local paths are not treated as opaque private
5464: folders: their bundle entries are directory file-manifests, so a reviewer can
5465: inspect the directory boundary and file list used by the row.  The remaining
5466: eleven entries are file snapshots.  Thus the review surface no longer depends on
5467: bare absolute paths alone.
5468: 
5469: The proposition's negative boundary follows from the same manifest entries and
5470: from Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion}.  The \path{boundary} field for each
5471: case records historical or path-verified evidence and explicitly withholds fresh
5472: daemon, full-source command, and Rule110 dynamic-rerun claims.  A directory
5473: manifest or copied file snapshot supplies reviewability of the cited surface; it
5474: does not supply command, commit, environment, exit-code, and log-path fields.
5475: Therefore the conclusion is exactly path-verified snapshot reviewability and not
5476: any stronger execution claim.\end{proof}
5477: 
5478: \begin{theorem}[Case-witness skeleton rigidity]
5479: \label{thm:case-witness-skeleton-rigidity}
5480: Let \(r\) be one of the four case-study rows in
5481: Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}, and assume the path-completeness and
5482: review-bundle coverage conclusions of
5483: Propositions~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses} and
5484: \ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}.  Then the accepted information
5485: carried by \(r\) is exactly one of the following four skeleton lessons and no
5486: other reproducibility or semantic-validation lesson:
5487: \begin{enumerate}
5488: \item a promotion skeleton: an intake packet is not daemon-visible publication
5489: work before human promotion;
5490: \item a route/submission skeleton: an overlap or submission-state gate blocks
5491: advancement until one of the recorded next actions occurs;
5492: \item a theorem-content skeleton: file churn or compilation does not by itself
5493: show substantive theorem growth or accepted discovery; or
5494: \item an artifact-limitation skeleton: a bounded artifact limitation must be
5495: disclosed or must block the corresponding artifact claim.
5496: \end{enumerate}
5497: Moreover this skeleton classification is rigid.  Any stronger reading of the
5498: same case row as a fresh daemon run, full-source rebuild, theorem-content
5499: rerun, artifact-suite rerun, benchmark, semantic artifact validation, or
5500: Rule110/GroundCompiler dynamic-validation result is accepted by the
5501: publication-safety interface if and only if the submitted package adds the
5502: positive extension coordinates required for that stronger reading: a new claim
5503: kind when needed, a submitted evidence surface for the run or semantic property,
5504: assigned pass rows for the enlarged gate set, and the command-run tuple
5505: containing command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path whenever
5506: fresh execution is asserted.  Without those coordinates, the case row remains
5507: accepted only at the path-verified historical-snapshot level and the stronger
5508: reading has an inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord.
5509: \end{theorem}
5510: 
5511: \begin{proof}
5512: By Proposition~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses}, a path-complete case row
5513: factors through exactly one of the four displayed finite witness skeletons:
5514: promotion, route/submission blocking, theorem-content non-growth, or
5515: artifact-limitation disclosure.  That proposition also identifies the unique
5516: admissible manuscript lesson attached to the skeleton.  By
5517: Proposition~\ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}, the local paths used
5518: by the row have a finite review-bundle surface: thirteen total local-path strings
5519: across the four rows, represented by copied snapshots or directory
5520: file-manifests.  Thus the evidence coordinate for \(r\) is a path-verified
5521: historical snapshot surface, not an executable event.  The four alternatives are
5522: exhaustive for the submitted case evidence because the finite-row closure theorem,
5523: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-row-case-evidence-closure}, maps the finite set of four
5524: case rows to exactly these four skeleton values and proves that no fresh rerun or
5525: semantic-validation value is accepted from the same row data.
5526: 
5527: Let \(r^{+}\) be a stronger reading of the same row.  If \(r^{+}\) asserts a
5528: fresh daemon run, full-source rebuild, theorem-content rerun, artifact-suite
5529: rerun, benchmark, or dynamic Rule110/GroundCompiler validation, it raises the
5530: evidence level from path-verified snapshot evidence to command-run evidence and
5531: therefore changes the boundary coordinate \(B\).  Clause~A6 of the finite
5532: certificate schema requires the command, source state, environment, exit code,
5533: and log path.  If \(r^{+}\) asserts a semantic artifact property rather than only
5534: a historical limitation lesson, it also adds an artifact-semantic evidence
5535: surface and the assigned pass record for the enlarged artifact claim kind.  If
5536: \(r^{+}\) asserts new theorem-content discovery, it adds the theorem-content or
5537: discovery component and the pinned discovery/interface pass rows.  These are
5538: precisely the positive extension coordinates listed by
5539: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.
5540: 
5541: If those coordinates are submitted and pass the gates assigned by \(\Gamma\), the
5542: enlarged tuple satisfies the schema clauses, and
5543: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} accepts the stronger finite-record
5544: reading at the declared level.  Conversely, if the stronger reading is accepted,
5545: the same exactness theorem forces every changed coordinate to be present and to
5546: pass the assigned gate checks.  When a required coordinate is absent, the failed
5547: schema clause supplies the minimal bad subrecord: missing submitted evidence,
5548: missing pass row, missing discovery-interface row, or missing command-run tuple.
5549: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} gives
5550: inclusion-minimality.  Hence the original row is rigid at its skeleton lesson and
5551: path-verified snapshot level, while fresh reproducibility or semantic-validation
5552: claims require a separately paid finite extension budget.\end{proof}
